spirit/run/process/js/lmStudioVisionProbe/results.json
spirit/currentIssues.md

# The appShellApp's granted subdomain names — a dataset a running app
# writes, inside a folder that ships. Andy: "A name grant persists. true.
# but only on the owners node." Committing one would put a node's grants
# in every clone, and worse in the lab: plantRun.js:56 builds each node
# home from `git ls-files --cached --others`, so a grants.json left by a
# test run would be PLANTED INTO EVERY NODE HOME. The durability
# assertion would then pass on a file the test never wrote, and "a second
# ask for the same name is refused" would go green or red depending on
# what the previous run granted. Caught by wsl-claude before the first
# push, 2026-09-25. The app folder still ships its manifest and its .js.
spirit/run/app/appShellApp/grants.json

# Personal media that ended up in a folder named test/pix rather than real
# test fixtures — unreferenced by any code or test (verified), 20MB.
spirit/test/pix

# Per-process relay state: routingTable.json, allow.json, and identity.json —
# the last of which holds an Ed25519 PRIVATE KEY. relayAuth.js's header has
# always said these "live on that process's spirit/run home, never in git",
# but until now nothing enforced it. This is not hypothetical once a relay
# runs on a public host and actually has an identity: a single `git add -A`
# would stage the key, and because both setupRelayFakes.js and labMaster's
# copyTrackedSpirit build node homes from `git ls-files`, a committed key
# would then be sprayed into every fake node in the lab.
# Unanchored on purpose — matches spirit/run/relay-state/ and any other
# node home's copy, wherever it ends up.
relay-state/

# Relay Chat's remembered binding (chat 1): which public label this node
# claimed, so a reload does not ask again. Per-node session state, like
# relay-state above and preferences.json — never product code, and
# meaningless in another clone. It also must not reach the lab:
# setupRelayFakes.js builds fake nodes from `git ls-files`, so a
# committed session.json would hand every fake node Andy's label.
# Unanchored, to match the file wherever an app folder ends up.
session.json

# Relay Chat's remembered view (chat 6): who was selected, which filter,
# and how far each conversation had been read. Same class as
# session.json above — this node's state about itself, never product
# code, and it names peer keys, so it must not reach the lab either.
view.json

# Relay Chat's settings (chat settings cycle): what this node has decided
# about mail from people it has not added, and the labels it has minted
# invites for. Same class as session.json and view.json above — this
# node's own state, meaningless in another clone, and it must not reach
# the lab either, since setupRelayFakes.js builds fake nodes from
# `git ls-files`.
prefs.json

# Scratch a person made while trying the Text Editor out. Not product
# code and not this node's state either — just a file somebody typed in
# to see whether saving worked. Unanchored, like the names above,
# because the next one will be made wherever the editor was pointed.
notes.txt

# The labels this node has minted invites for (natterRememberMinted).
# Same class as session.json, view.json and prefs.json above: this node's
# own state, meaningless in another clone. It also names people — a label
# is what somebody will be called on a mailbox once they claim — and it
# must not reach the lab, since setupRelayFakes.js builds fake nodes from
# `git ls-files`.
#
# The token is deliberately NOT in it: that is the secret, spoken down a
# phone and never written here. This is the half that lets an owner
# recognise the key when it turns up on their census.
minted.json

# Anything js/peerFile.js names: one file per peer, wherever an app
# keeps them. Chat logs are the first (app/relayChat/logs/, also caught
# by the bare `logs` rule above); whoBook sidecars and whatever comes
# next are the reason this is a pattern rather than a path.
#
# Data belonging to a peer is personal by definition — the mailbox keeps
# messages by recipient and grows no sender copy, so the record of a
# conversation lives on the node that took part in it. It must not reach
# the lab either: setupRelayFakes.js builds fake nodes from
# `git ls-files`.
#
# The prefix is what makes one line enough, and it is why session.json
# is listed separately above: that is one file about this node, not a
# file about a peer.
**/peerfile-*.json

# THIS NODE'S OWN RELAY LIST. Same class as session.json above and
# ignored for the same reason: it is what one box has decided about where
# it keeps its mail, and it is meaningless in another clone.
#
#   Andy: "i want all my nodes on labMaster page to only be updated via
#   github, jazz's binding to spirit-3 keeps getting trashed."
#
# It was TRACKED, and that is the file that fights a `git pull`. A lab
# node bound to spirit-3 has a relays.json of its own; `git reset --hard
# origin/master` — which is how the public relay updates itself
# (install/kamerata/update.sh) — reverts it, and the node comes back
# pointing somewhere else. Every other piece of a node's state survives
# an update because git never had it. This one did not, alone.
#
# It had already cost once from the other direction: labPopulate rewrites
# it during a lab run, so a reflexive `git add -A` ships a loopback relay
# as everybody's default.
#
# WHAT A FRESH CLONE LOSES is a list of relays it has never spoken to.
# That is not a loss: an unbound node showing Natter's add-a-relay row is
# what an unbound node is, and a list shipped in git was the box
# pretending to know somewhere it had never been.
spirit/run/app/natter/relays.json

# Written by labMaster (and any future packaging step) so a copied tree
# can say which commit it came from. Never a checkout, so never tracked.
spirit/run/build.json

# Transient: labPopulate copies relays.json here before putting a lab
# row in front of it, and --down restores from it. Never a committed file —
# staged once by reflex, it made git ls-files name a path that the teardown
# had already deleted, and every lab node then failed to build.
spirit/run/app/natter/relays.json.before-lab
# And the binding, backed up the same way for a better reason: session.json
# is what firstRun() reads, so a node that loses it opens to Natter alone
# and behaves like a stranger to itself with its identity, contacts, relays
# and mail all still there. Ignored for the same hard reason as the line
# above — missingFromCopy uses --exclude-standard, so an untracked backup
# refuses every lab build until it is deleted.
spirit/run/app/natter/session.json.before-lab
NEXT_PROMPT.md

# Andy's own project, not a node feature and not a SpiritOS concept: a
# private GitHub repo he keeps inside his personal spirit-FS — raw input
# he writes, a wiki compiled from it — for his idea of training a future
# robot-self. A user-app for the shell may grow out of it one day. Until
# that day the product does not know this folder exists: no gate names it,
# no schema describes it, no test asserts it, and nothing in js/ reads it.
#
# It is ignored for the plain reason that it is a SEPARATE REPO with its
# own remote, so the outer tree must never descend into it — and for the
# same hard reason as every name above: setupRelayFakes.js builds fake
# nodes from `git ls-files`, and a fake node holding Andy's private notes
# is the worst version of that mistake.
#
# Being inside ROOT_DIR means the shell can read it, and that is not an
# oversight to fix. This is personal content in a personal filesystem,
# exactly like media/ and published/ — one operator, loopback only, and
# every app in the shell is Andy's. If a brains app is ever built it will
# WANT that read. The day an untrusted app can run here, this folder is
# one of several that need an answer, and it is not the special case.
#
# Anchored by its slashes on purpose, unlike relay-state/ above: that one
# is unanchored to catch every node home's copy, and this one must NOT be
# — the vault is singular and belongs to the person, so a lab home that
# grew a `brains` folder would be a bug to see, not a path to hide.
spirit/run/brains/

# ── A RESTARTED NODE'S CONSOLE, WHEREVER IT IS RESTARTED FROM ────────
#
# Starting a node detached needs somewhere for its stdout and stderr to
# go, and the obvious place is beside the clone it runs from. Those two
# files are the node's console and nothing else — they are recreated on
# every start and mean nothing to anybody who was not watching that
# process. Added 2026-09-24 after rule 11b's first application left them
# in `git status` in two checkouts.
#
# It matters more here than the size of the files suggests: this
# repository stages by name and never `git add -A`, and untracked noise
# in the root is exactly what makes somebody reach for the shortcut.
node.out
node.err

# Per-app state (G12): code is replaced by deployment, state is not.
# Same reason relay-state is ignored above.
app-state/

# What one box says about itself (see .spiritbox.example). Machine facts,
# never shared: the file exists so a box can stop re-typing them.
.spiritbox
